  },
  {
    "episode_id": 91062,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91062.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Well, I know when people were looking at my television commercials, there was no mystery as to what my race was, right? And so ultimately, I had to focus on the things that people care about. Will Washington work for them? Will the people that I've known my whole life in Rhode Island, in the first congressional district, have someone who builds on the legacy of so many in our congressional delegations past to bring resources back home? would they have somebody who could really cut through noise and you know by partisan fashion go and try to get some things done that's what ultimately people want to hear from me and I do mean it I like ran to make a difference not to make history of course I did that along the way I'm grateful for Rhode Islanders for seeing me for all the parts that I am someone with great experience in government and someone who has a slightly different story. But what you heard me say often is we all are sharing the same Rhode Island story where someone came before us and worked to make our lives better. And we have that sacred obligation to move forward. And that's what I'm doing.",
