    "turn_text": "Yeah, I think it's a decision that you have to be able to explain to your constituents. And if you do that, then you should have a clear conscience about it. For me, I know that people in the first district want me to listen to views that I agree with and views that I disagree with. I don't believe the Benjamin Netanyahu has been an effective leader, especially amidst this conflict. And I had to hear it directly. I thought it was important to sit in the chamber. I said, remember, I sit in that chamber with a lot of people I disagree with often. And I thought it was very important for me to listen and respond accordingly. And I think what was missing in a very big way was any commitment to a deal, to a ceasefire deal that's on the table so we can have a path forward so we can get hostages back home. Remember, hostages have only returned when there's been a deal. Moreover, we have to end the suffering that so many people, so many Palestinians by no choice of their own are experiencing. And you know, we're reading reports about polio spreading in Gaza. And I did not hear that sympathy for that condition. We've got to get everybody to the table here, and I'm going to spend as much of my time trying to think about the day after and how we can end cycles of",
