    "turn_idx": 58,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, just short of nine months since I've been in Congress. And I couldn't have picked a more eventful time to be in Congress. Everything from expelling colleagues to voting to keep the government open once, twice, three times, to major aid packages that we've had to focus on for our national security. So look, I think the thing that I'm most surprised by is the fact that you've got some people there who really want the institution to fail. on the other side of the aisle. It is unfortunate to me because I want this to work. And I do believe there are more people who want it to work than not. But when you have, whether it's appropriations bills filled with poison pills around all these polarizing, controversial things that aren't about actually solving any problems, you know, it's It's one thing to see it on TV, it's another thing to see it on the house floor. I would say beyond that, I think that surprises me in a positive way, is that there are some of the greatest public servants in our country there. And when you spend some time on both sides of the aisle, you learn things about yourself. And I remain positive, I remain optimistic, and I want to continue to work for Rhode",
